A||||2||FieroGT||Just purchased ScanTool......||||14||
Z||000000||FieroGT||04-17-2000||03:33 PM||||I just purchased a ScanTool, made by SunPro. Unit cost me $160 from Autozone and I had to purchase the proper cartridge which covers GM '84-'95, for $80. For a combined $240 investment, I have found that this is really the way to go. I've meant to get one of these for about 4 years now, and have finally fone it, since I have a few cars that can work with this tool. It lets you view all engine parameters as seen by the ECM thru the ALDL connector. Sure saves time and money instead of throwing parts at it. Only problem I had was that I had to get 4 units before I found one that operated correctly, since they are usually manhandled at the retail stores. You have to have a good factory service manual to get the most out of it, though. Cerain data it gives you will be useless without having normal parameters to compare it to. Anyone else have positive experiences with these thigs?||||reg||14
Z||000001||mwbackus||04-17-2000||04:22 PM||||A simple search of the forum could have saved you bunch of cash.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>The AutoXray performs the same functions, but can be found at places like Checker Auto for around $150 (complete with the GM specific cable).<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-17-2000||06:45 PM||||Factory book needed? not really.<P>The main things you are looking for are temp sensors that are out of range, TPS jitter, TPS over/under volt, Fan 1 on/off (87+DIS 4's only).... a few other things. <P>I don't know your particular tool.... AutoXray is pretty self explanitory and its book is pretty usefull.<P>With temp sensors you are looking for things like coolant saying 90 when it's more like 190. TPS values that change but you aint pushin the pedal.<P>You often can't work without the scan tool but you often need other tools as well. A good analog volt/ohm meter. (Digital works but it's a *****  to read on variable sensors.) A digital pyrometer, K thermocouple type, is handy when working on temp sensors. If you get the thermocouple that's just a dot on the end of the lead you can use it to check ACT/MAT sensors in use. (It's about the only way to verify those sensors are correct.)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-17-2000).]||||reg||1
